WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.966
Hi, and welcome.

00:00:00.966 --> 00:00:04.165
The eco-club wants a camera to sort lunch packaging.

00:00:04.165 --> 00:00:12.435
It works on the practice photos, but the club has not yet checked whether it learned the objects or the table they were photographed on.

00:00:12.435 --> 00:00:22.033
As you watch, keep one question in mind: When a prediction surprises you, ask what pattern the system may have used and what new example would help you check.

00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:24.750
You do not need to memorize a list of terms.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:31.752
Try to notice what the people in the story know, what they still need to check, and what choice they can make next.

00:00:32.302 --> 00:00:36.810
Think of a new student learning the recycling routine by watching examples.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:43.662
They can copy a pattern without knowing why it works, and a different cafeteria can make that pattern fall apart.

00:00:43.662 --> 00:00:47.028
This comparison is a way to think, not a perfect match.

00:00:47.028 --> 00:00:53.579
Real AI systems can work in different ways, so it helps to ask what this particular tool was designed to do.

00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:56.344
Separate a fixed rule from a learned pattern.

00:00:56.344 --> 00:00:59.710
What detail in the story makes that goal easier to see?

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.709
A reusable bottle is made of metal, but it is not a can.

00:01:03.709 --> 00:01:10.175
One example like this can expose a shortcut that seemed reliable when every training photo was familiar.

00:01:10.175 --> 00:01:11.407
Pause for a moment.

00:01:11.407 --> 00:01:14.917
What changed between the first situation and the second?

00:01:14.917 --> 00:01:18.797
If the result changed too, that is a clue worth investigating.

00:01:18.797 --> 00:01:20.891
It is not proof of the cause yet.

00:01:20.891 --> 00:01:26.372
Think of one more example that could help separate a genuine pattern from a coincidence.

00:01:26.922 --> 00:01:32.559
A shiny wrapper appears in bright light, then the same wrapper looks dull under a shadow.

00:01:32.559 --> 00:01:37.436
The object stayed the same; the information available to the camera changed.

00:01:37.436 --> 00:01:40.856
A useful explanation connects a detail to a decision.

00:01:40.856 --> 00:01:46.872
Try finishing this sentence in your own words: I would pay attention to this detail because...

00:01:46.872 --> 00:01:50.166
Identify features, labels, training, and inference.

00:01:50.166 --> 00:01:53.966
You can use a different example from the one in the lesson.

00:01:53.966 --> 00:01:58.146
The goal is to explain the reasoning, not to repeat a definition.

00:01:58.696 --> 00:02:02.390
Try the sorter with new objects, backgrounds, and lighting.

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:09.715
Keep some examples aside until the end, so the final check is not just a repeat of what the system has already seen.

00:02:09.715 --> 00:02:14.034
Before looking at any result, say what you expect to happen and why.

00:02:14.034 --> 00:02:17.603
Then compare your prediction with what actually happens.

00:02:17.603 --> 00:02:23.488
If they do not match, keep the surprising result; it may be the most useful part of the test.

00:02:24.038 --> 00:02:28.167
Now imagine two objects that look alike but belong in different bins.

00:02:28.167 --> 00:02:33.313
What extra evidence could help, and what would be a guess the camera should not make?

00:02:33.313 --> 00:02:37.083
It is tempting to judge a tool by its most impressive example.

00:02:37.083 --> 00:02:43.665
A more careful check also asks who was not represented, what information is missing, and what could go wrong.

00:02:43.665 --> 00:02:46.478
Explain why a prediction still needs checking.

00:02:46.478 --> 00:02:50.846
A person should be able to question a result and choose what to do next.

00:02:51.396 --> 00:02:56.592
Suppose the camera labels a bottle as a can only when it sits on the blue lunch tray.

00:02:56.592 --> 00:03:01.359
Change one detail at a time, then ask which change makes the prediction move.

00:03:01.359 --> 00:03:03.315
Work through it in three moves.

00:03:03.315 --> 00:03:06.066
First, say what job the tool is meant to do.

00:03:06.066 --> 00:03:11.262
Next, point to the evidence you have, rather than a guess about how the tool thinks.

00:03:11.262 --> 00:03:14.196
Finally, decide what still needs a human check.

00:03:14.196 --> 00:03:18.780
There may be more than one reasonable answer if you explain your evidence.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:24.421
Now open the practice activity, Build the recycling pipeline, when you are ready.

00:03:24.421 --> 00:03:31.839
As you work through the recycling activity, separate what the system receives from what a person wants it to predict.

00:03:31.839 --> 00:03:34.039
Those are easy to mix up at first.

00:03:34.039 --> 00:03:36.616
Take a first try before asking for help.

00:03:36.616 --> 00:03:40.639
If feedback changes your mind, name the detail that changed it.

00:03:40.639 --> 00:03:44.410
If you keep your answer, explain what evidence supports it.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.492
Your lesson project is called Design your own school helper.

00:03:48.492 --> 00:03:54.144
For your project, picture one real school task and one new example that could fool your design.

00:03:54.144 --> 00:03:58.559
A useful proposal explains what a person would do when the tool is unsure.

00:03:58.559 --> 00:04:03.033
Before you finish, imagine someone else using your idea for the first time.

00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:04.799
What would they need to know?

00:04:04.799 --> 00:04:07.978
What could they do if the tool did not work for them?

00:04:07.978 --> 00:04:11.216
Those questions can make a promising idea more useful.

00:04:11.766 --> 00:04:16.849
Pick one word from today's lesson and explain it using the story you just heard.

00:04:16.849 --> 00:04:19.581
Avoid copying the wording from the screen.

00:04:19.581 --> 00:04:23.076
You might begin, In this situation, this word means...

00:04:23.076 --> 00:04:26.888
If your explanation is hard to follow, add a small example.

00:04:26.888 --> 00:04:32.670
Clear language is a good way to notice what you understand and what you want to ask about.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:41.782
Here is one idea to take with you: When a prediction surprises you, ask what pattern the system may have used and what new example would help you check.

00:04:41.782 --> 00:04:46.457
Think of a different class or school situation where the same question could help.

00:04:46.457 --> 00:04:50.681
You can write down one thing you would check and one person you could ask.

00:04:50.681 --> 00:04:55.300
That is enough for a useful next step; you do not have to solve every part today.

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:58.227
Thanks for thinking this through with me.

00:04:58.227 --> 00:05:04.024
Finish the activity and project on the lesson page, then try the separate quiz when you feel ready.

00:05:04.024 --> 00:05:09.764
If a question feels difficult, return to the example and explain your reasoning in your own words.

00:05:09.764 --> 00:05:16.605
When a prediction surprises you, ask what pattern the system may have used and what new example would help you check.

00:05:16.605 --> 00:05:18.866
The goal is not to trust every answer.

00:05:18.866 --> 00:05:22.634
It is to know what to check and how to make a thoughtful choice.
